== C3a: Var(int_0^tau W^2 dt) = tau^4/3 and E exp(b int W^2) = cos(tau sqrt(2b))^(-1/2) (Cameron-Martin) ==
  mean 0.4989 (1/2)  var 0.3312 (1/3)  E e^(0.3 I)=1.1822 vs 1.1829
== C3b: survival-optimal tau maximizes the Lundberg exponent theta(tau); diffusion approx theta ~ 2 m/s^2 ==
  EOQ (mean-cost) tau* = 0.316;  survival-optimal tau* (exact Lundberg) = 0.258;  (diffusion approx) = 0.269
   tau=0.150: margin m=0.5917  s^2=0.2511  theta_exact=4.7109  theta_diff=4.7121
   tau=0.258: margin m=0.6771  s^2=0.2557  theta_exact=5.2682  theta_diff=5.2960
   tau=0.316: margin m=0.6838  s^2=0.2605  theta_exact=5.1639  theta_diff=5.2489
   tau=0.600: margin m=0.6167  s^2=0.3220  theta_exact=2.8066  theta_diff=3.8302
  => survival-optimal checking is FASTER than cost-optimal when loss variance grows with tau (here s^2 has a tau^3 term).
  => argmax_tau theta does not involve B: under Lundberg asymptotics the optimal check interval is independent of the buffer.
== C3c: Monte Carlo ruin vs exp(-theta B) (cycle-level walk, exact cycle increments sampled from simulated I) ==
  tau=0.258 B=0.5: MC ruin=0.0334  exp(-theta B)=0.0718 (Lundberg bound; Cramer constant <1)
  tau=0.258 B=1.0: MC ruin=0.0024  exp(-theta B)=0.0052 (Lundberg bound; Cramer constant <1)
  tau=0.258 B=2.0: MC ruin=0.0000  exp(-theta B)=0.0000 (Lundberg bound; Cramer constant <1)
  tau=0.600 B=0.5: MC ruin=0.0634  exp(-theta B)=0.2458 (Lundberg bound; Cramer constant <1)
  tau=0.600 B=1.0: MC ruin=0.0144  exp(-theta B)=0.0604 (Lundberg bound; Cramer constant <1)
  tau=0.600 B=2.0: MC ruin=0.0008  exp(-theta B)=0.0036 (Lundberg bound; Cramer constant <1)
== C3d: value of improving theta when ruin costs V: d/dtheta [V e^{-theta B}] = -V B e^{-theta B}; maximized over B at theta*B = 1 ==
  argmax_B B e^{-theta B} = [1/theta]
  => effort to improve survival is worth most when the buffer is ~one ruin length 1/theta = s^2/(2m):
     'watch most where shocks are large relative to buffers but not yet overwhelming them' (rule 7, last clause) follows here.
